BERM is covered by two licences, because code and documentation want
different things from a licence.

  Code                    GPL-3.0        see LICENSE-GPL-3.0.txt
  Documentation,          CC BY-SA 4.0   see LICENSE-CC-BY-SA-4.0.txt
  hardware designs,
  and written material

Both are share-alike, which is the point: credit the original author, and
release derivative works under the same licence. GPL-3.0 is the software
equivalent of CC BY-SA — Creative Commons themselves point to it for code,
because CC licences say nothing useful about linking, derivative works, or
patents.

Share-alike here costs a builder almost nothing. BERM Bridge is a separate
process with an HTTP API, so nobody links it into their program — they talk
to it over localhost. If you are writing a game, a visualizer, a patch, or a
commercial product that READS from Bridge, the GPL does not reach your code
at all. It binds only someone who modifies Bridge itself and redistributes
it, and then only to share those modifications back.

Everything in bridge/ is GPL-3.0. The README and any docs are CC BY-SA 4.0.
